<model>
    <mount>//OPNsense/XGS</mount>
    <version>1.3.0</version>
    <description>XGS coprocessor</description>
    <items>
        <!--
            The settings an owner decides, and only those.
            Everything else the coprocessor can be told is reachable only through the rpc sysctls,
            which are instruments: they stage a request, they have no idea what the firewall is
            doing, and a wrong value in one of them is a dropped LAN rather than a rejected form.
            They do not belong behind a save button. What is here is either a gate the owner is
            entitled to open (offload, flow_auto, ipsec_offload, ipsec_flows), a boot behaviour
            (switch_jumbo, ipsec_soft_crypto) or a measured tuning of the receive path (rx_poll_ms,
            rx_budget). scripts/xgs/apply.php writes them where each one takes effect - the
            coprocessor, a running sysctl, or a loader tunable through OPNsense's own Tunables -
            and bringup.sh, 07-npuep and switch-jumbo.sh read them back at boot by exactly these
            paths, so the names here are not free. A field one family does not have is ignored there.
        -->
        <general>
            <offload type="BooleanField">
                <Required>Y</Required>
                <!--
                    On in a new install since 2026-11-14 (the owner's decision D9), with the flow
                    maker below; off until then.

                    The coprocessor comes up with fw_cfg 0x2 - TCP_SEQ_CHK alone, no offload - and
                    forgets the gate at every boot, so this is written again at every boot
                    (bringup.sh, 07-npuep-gate). With the gate open, a frame the fast path decides it
                    can handle does not reach pf; opening it by itself accelerates nothing, because a
                    frame leaves the host's path only once the driver has put its connection in
                    hardware, which the flow maker does (docs/findings/the-state-is-the-gate.md).

                    It was off for as long as a connection in hardware could escape the Intrusion
                    Detection: a port Suricata reads through netmap lost the end of every transfer
                    the flow maker cut to hardware (issue 1148). The netmap gate (dp.netmap_gate,
                    flow.netmap_gate) now keeps every connection on such a port with the host, so
                    the default changed with it, and only with it.

                    A default is what a new install gets: an installed appliance keeps the value it
                    saved, and XGS: Overview offers the recommended values to its owner
                    (Api/SettingsController.php, recommended). Never a migration.
                -->
                <Default>1</Default>
            </offload>
            <!-- The flow maker: dp.auto on the OCTEON TX, flow.auto on the ARMADA. On in a new install
                 since 2026-11-14, with offload above and for its reasons: it is what takes a
                 connection out of the firewall's path, and the netmap gate keeps the ports Suricata
                 reads in IPS mode with the host. -->
            <flow_auto type="BooleanField">
                <Required>Y</Required>
                <Default>1</Default>
            </flow_auto>
            <!-- The coprocessor's own counts of what it forwards, given to pf's states and rules
                 (dev.octep.0.dp.hw_to_pf, dev.npuep.0.flow.hw_to_pf) and to the interfaces' counters
                 (dp.lif_apply, flow.lif_apply), when the page is saved and at every boot
                 (scripts/octep/bringup.sh, rc.syshook.d/early/07-npuep). On by default: both were
                 measured to the packet on the XGS 3300 (docs/measurements/hw-counters-2026-10-19.md). -->
            <count_to_pf type="BooleanField">
                <Required>Y</Required>
                <Default>1</Default>
            </count_to_pf>
            <count_ports type="BooleanField">
                <Required>Y</Required>
                <Default>1</Default>
            </count_ports>
            <!-- What the owner keeps with the host: addresses, networks and ports never offloaded,
                 written to dp.flow_exclude or flow.exclude by scripts/xgs/flow_exclude.php when the
                 page is saved and at every boot. Only the characters are checked here; the script
                 reads each entry and names one it cannot read. -->
            <flow_exclude type="TextField">
                <Required>N</Required>
                <Mask>/^[0-9A-Za-z:.\/,;\s]{0,1500}$/</Mask>
                <ValidationMessage>Addresses, networks and "port N" entries, separated by commas or new lines.</ValidationMessage>
            </flow_exclude>
            <!-- Captive Portal clients in hardware (discovery 5, 2026-10-24): scripts/xgs/cp_users.py,
                 every two seconds from cron.d/xgs-cp, lists each portal session's addresses as a live
                 user (dp.live_users, flow.live_users), so a logout takes its connections out of
                 hardware at once; flow_exclude.php then stops keeping those zones with the host. A
                 zone with an idle timeout or RADIUS accounting needs the counts cp_overlay.py adds to
                 the portal's, and stays with the host where the overlay is not on. On in a new install
                 since 2026-11-14 (svc-2-16), off until then. -->
            <cp_hardware type="BooleanField">
                <Required>Y</Required>
                <Default>1</Default>
            </cp_hardware>
            <!-- Block lists whose additions cut the connections already open: scripts/xgs/lists_cut.py,
                 every minute from cron.d/xgs-lists and when the page is saved. The aliases are pf tables;
                 the internal ones (__lan_network and the like) are not offered - cutting one would cut
                 a whole network - nor the port and MAC kinds, which hold no addresses. -->
            <cut_lists type="ModelRelationField">
                <Model>
                    <aliases>
                        <source>OPNsense.Firewall.Alias</source>
                        <items>aliases.alias</items>
                        <display>name</display>
                        <filters>
                            <type>/^(?!port$|mac$).*/</type>
                            <name>/^(?!__).*/</name>
                        </filters>
                    </aliases>
                </Model>
                <Multiple>Y</Multiple>
                <Required>N</Required>
            </cut_lists>
            <!-- TLS fingerprints (JA4) whose connections are cut: scripts/xgs/ja4_cut.py reads the
                 Intrusion Detection's eve.json every two seconds (cron.d/xgs-ja4) and when the page is
                 saved. Each entry is checked here, so the script never meets one it cannot read. -->
            <cut_ja4 type="TextField">
                <Required>N</Required>
                <Mask>/^[\s,]*([tqd][0-9a-z]{2}[di][0-9]{4}[0-9A-Za-z]{2}_[0-9a-f]{12}_[0-9a-f]{12}([\s,]+[tqd][0-9a-z]{2}[di][0-9]{4}[0-9A-Za-z]{2}_[0-9a-f]{12}_[0-9a-f]{12}){0,199})?[\s,]*$/</Mask>
                <ValidationMessage>JA4 fingerprints, as t13d1516h2_8daaf6152771_e5627efa2ab1, separated by commas or new lines.</ValidationMessage>
            </cut_ja4>
            <!-- Cut in hardware (discovery 3, 2026-10-23): plain connections made with the microflow
                 action IPS and the fast path's IPS switch on (dp.mflow_action / flow.mflow_action,
                 offload.sh), so a connection the two cuts above cut is dropped on the coprocessor by
                 its verdict (scripts/xgs/hwdrop.py) before its pf state is killed. Measured to cost
                 nothing; on by default, the owner's choice of the same day. -->
            <cut_in_hardware type="BooleanField">
                <Required>Y</Required>
                <Default>1</Default>
            </cut_in_hardware>
            <!-- Flood cut (svc-2-05): the pf tables OPNsense's own rules fill when a source passes a
                 rule's connection limits - virusprot, and a rule's own overload table - watched by
                 scripts/xgs/lists_cut.py like a block list, so what such a source has open is cut,
                 in hardware first where "Cut in hardware" is on. The coprocessor's DoS table cannot
                 do it: it dispatches none of its commands (docs/measurements/dos-probe-2026-10-19.md).
                 On in a new install since 2026-11-14. -->
            <flood_cut type="BooleanField">
                <Required>Y</Required>
                <Default>1</Default>
            </flood_cut>
            <!-- Inspect, then hardware (discovery 4, 2026-10-23): the marker
                 /var/db/os-xgs-npu/inspector-vf, which has the driver make the coprocessor's
                 inspector function at boot; dp.ips_new / flow.ips_new; and oxips0 added to the
                 Intrusion Detection by the template overlay OPNsense/IDS/xgs-inspector.yaml. On in a
                 new install since 2026-11-14 (svc-2-06), off until then. It does nothing while the
                 Intrusion Detection is off or in IDS mode: nothing reads oxips0, and the drivers hand
                 each frame straight back and set its connection to forward (failclose leaves them
                 so). The inspector's function is made at the boot after it is first on. -->
            <inspect_then_hardware type="BooleanField">
                <Required>Y</Required>
                <Default>1</Default>
            </inspect_then_hardware>
            <!-- Let rejected TCP segments through (issue 1148, measured 2026-11-12): while "Inspect,
                 then hardware" is on, the template overlay OPNsense/IDS/xgs-inspector.yaml writes
                 stream.drop-invalid: no, so the closing frames of a connection cut to hardware are
                 not dropped by Suricata when it never identified the connection's protocol (and so
                 never bypassed it). Off by default: Suricata's own behaviour. -->
            <inspect_pass_invalid type="BooleanField">
                <Required>Y</Required>
                <Default>0</Default>
            </inspect_pass_invalid>
            <!-- Drop when the inspection stops (svc-5-04): while "Inspect, then hardware" is on and
                 the Intrusion Detection is enabled in IPS mode, a frame the fast path sends the
                 inspector with nothing reading oxips0 is dropped (dp.ips_loop 0, ips.loop 0) instead
                 of passed with its connection set to forward. Any other time the drivers' own 1
                 stands, so a fresh install with the Intrusion Detection off drops nothing.
                 scripts/xgs/failclose.py keeps the knob in step with the configuration (apply, boot,
                 and every two seconds from cron.d/xgs-failclose). On in a new install since
                 2026-11-14; an appliance installed before gets 0 from Migrations/M1_3_0.php, its
                 behaviour until then. -->
            <failclose type="BooleanField">
                <Required>Y</Required>
                <Default>1</Default>
            </failclose>
            <!-- Copies for packet capture (discoveries 12 and 17, 2026-10-25): the fast path's
                 FP_PKT_DUMP bit, 0x10 of its configuration word, written by offload.sh with the gate;
                 the copies it then sends (control message type 6) go to the port's BPF in the
                 drivers (octep_dp_mirror, npugiu_mirror). Off by default: a way to look. -->
            <capture_copies type="BooleanField">
                <Required>Y</Required>
                <Default>0</Default>
            </capture_copies>
            <!-- Unusual traffic reported, never blocked: scripts/xgs/anomaly.py, every minute from
                 cron.d/xgs-anomaly, which ends at once while this is off. Each alert also goes to
                 /var/db/os-xgs-npu/anomaly/alerts.jsonl for os-netreport to send. On in a new install
                 since 2026-11-14 (svc-2-23), off until then. -->
            <anomaly_watch type="BooleanField">
                <Required>Y</Required>
                <Default>1</Default>
            </anomaly_watch>
            <!-- IPsec associations to the coprocessor's engine: the loader tunable hw.octep.ipsec_on
                 or hw.npuep.ipsec_on, read when the driver attaches - a reboot takes it. On in a new
                 install since 2026-11-14 (hw-02); the tunable is written by the first apply of the
                 settings page, and the reboot after it takes it. -->
            <ipsec_offload type="BooleanField">
                <Required>Y</Required>
                <Default>1</Default>
            </ipsec_offload>
            <!-- An association's flows forwarded by the coprocessor itself: hw.octep.ipsec_flows=1
                 on the OCTEON TX, hw.npuep.ipsec_flows=1 on the ARMADA (since 2026-10-20). The
                 OCTEON TX driver's value 2 is never offered: it forwards matching clear text that
                 arrives on the tunnel's port. On in a new install since 2026-11-14, with
                 ipsec_offload, and taken by a reboot as it is. -->
            <ipsec_flows type="BooleanField">
                <Required>Y</Required>
                <Default>1</Default>
            </ipsec_flows>
            <!-- ARMADA only: keep IPsec's software sessions off the host's AMD ccp(4) (issue 399). -->
            <ipsec_soft_crypto type="BooleanField">
                <Required>Y</Required>
                <Default>1</Default>
            </ipsec_soft_crypto>
            <!-- ARMADA only: the receive poll, measured in docs/findings/a-hundred-polls-a-second-on-the-armada.md. -->
            <rx_poll_ms type="IntegerField">
                <Required>Y</Required>
                <Default>1</Default>
                <MinimumValue>1</MinimumValue>
                <MaximumValue>100</MaximumValue>
            </rx_poll_ms>
            <rx_budget type="IntegerField">
                <Required>Y</Required>
                <Default>1024</Default>
                <MinimumValue>4</MinimumValue>
                <MaximumValue>4096</MaximumValue>
            </rx_budget>
            <!-- ARMADA only: the receive queues on the coprocessor's doorbells, measured in
                 docs/measurements/rx-msix-136-2026-10-20.md; the loader tunable hw.npuep.giu.rx_msix. -->
            <rx_doorbells type="BooleanField">
                <Required>Y</Required>
                <Default>1</Default>
            </rx_doorbells>
            <!--
                The flow table's size and the transmit queues, both read by the driver when it
                attaches (docs/families/board-limits.md). 0 is the driver's own choice - the board's
                figure capped by a 16th of memory, and as many queues as CPUs - and removes the
                tunable rather than writing 0, which the driver would take as its minimum.
            -->
            <flow_max type="IntegerField">
                <Required>Y</Required>
                <Default>0</Default>
                <MinimumValue>0</MinimumValue>
                <MaximumValue>2000000</MaximumValue>
            </flow_max>
            <tx_queues type="IntegerField">
                <Required>Y</Required>
                <Default>0</Default>
                <MinimumValue>0</MinimumValue>
                <MaximumValue>7</MaximumValue>
            </tx_queues>
            <!-- Jumbo frames through the switch chip at every boot (scripts/xgs/switch-jumbo.sh). -->
            <switch_jumbo type="BooleanField">
                <Required>Y</Required>
                <Default>1</Default>
            </switch_jumbo>
            <!--
                The host's hardware watchdog (scripts/xgs/watchdog.sh): watchdogd at every boot, so a
                host that stops scheduling is reset by the timer instead of waiting for somebody to
                pull the power. On by default since 2026-10-17.
            -->
            <watchdog type="BooleanField">
                <Required>Y</Required>
                <Default>1</Default>
            </watchdog>
            <!--
                When the coprocessor's fast path stops answering its heartbeat (scripts/xgs/fastpath.py):
                1, the default since 2026-10-26, takes the coprocessor up again in place with the host
                running (scripts/xgs/recover.sh), and restarts the appliance if that fails (#768);
                0 says so - the system log and a problem report - and nothing more; 2, 5 or 10 restarts
                the appliance once it has gone that many minutes without an answer, at most three times
                a day. Until 2026-10-26 the default was 0, as the vendor's own host leaves its panic for
                this switched off - and an XGS 3300's host froze as its coprocessor reset.
            -->
            <fastpath_restart type="OptionField">
                <Required>Y</Required>
                <Default>1</Default>
                <OptionValues>
                    <option1 value="1">Take it up again, or restart</option1>
                    <option0 value="0">Say so, and nothing more</option0>
                    <option2 value="2">Restart the appliance after 2 minutes</option2>
                    <option5 value="5">Restart the appliance after 5 minutes</option5>
                    <option10 value="10">Restart the appliance after 10 minutes</option10>
                </OptionValues>
            </fastpath_restart>
            <!--
                Power over Ethernet: the front ports whose power the owner switched off, by their
                labels ("11,12" on the XGS 136), set from the Ports page (Api/PoeController.php) and
                at every boot by scripts/xgs/poe.py. Every other PoE port stays on, which is how the
                controller starts. Empty, so nothing is switched off by being installed.
            -->
            <poe_off type="TextField">
                <Required>N</Required>
                <Mask>/^([0-9]{1,2}(,[0-9]{1,2}){0,15})?$/</Mask>
                <ValidationMessage>Front port labels, separated by commas.</ValidationMessage>
            </poe_off>
            <!--
                Power over Ethernet: the front ports held to 15.4 W (IEEE 802.3af, "AF") instead of
                30 W (802.3at, "AT"), by their labels, set from the Ports page and at every boot by
                scripts/xgs/poe.py: the controller starts every port at AT. The vendor's guide holds
                one port to 15.4 W when a high-performance expansion module is fitted. Empty, so every
                port keeps 30 W.
            -->
            <poe_af type="TextField">
                <Required>N</Required>
                <Mask>/^([0-9]{1,2}(,[0-9]{1,2}){0,15})?$/</Mask>
                <ValidationMessage>Front port labels, separated by commas.</ValidationMessage>
            </poe_af>
        </general>
        <!--
            Problem reports (scripts/xgs/reporter.py, docs/problem-reports.md): what goes wrong on
            this appliance, cleaned of addresses and serials, filed as a GitHub issue through the
            project's relay. Manual by default since 1.2.0 (2026-10-23): each report waits until its
            Send is pressed, and nothing leaves the appliance unless its owner chooses so - XGS:
            Overview asks once. Automatic, the default until then, sends each report as it is
            found; off looks at nothing.
        -->
        <reporting>
            <mode type="OptionField">
                <Required>Y</Required>
                <Default>manual</Default>
                <OptionValues>
                    <automatic>Automatic: send each report as it is found</automatic>
                    <manual>Manual: keep each report until I send it</manual>
                    <off>Off: look for nothing and send nothing</off>
                </OptionValues>
            </mode>
            <!-- Kept by the relay with the issue's number, never in the issue itself. -->
            <email type="EmailField">
                <Required>N</Required>
            </email>
            <!-- This appliance counted on npuforge.org's home page once a day (scripts/xgs/count.py):
                 a random identifier it made for itself, its model and its release. Off unless the
                 owner turns it on, since 1.2.0 (2026-10-23); on by default from 2026-10-20 until then. -->
            <count type="BooleanField">
                <Required>Y</Required>
                <Default>0</Default>
            </count>
        </reporting>
    </items>
</model>
